  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/c1/Avengers_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Hulk, Thor, and Iron Man await the arrival of Ant-Man and the Wasp at their next Avengers meeting, which is to take place in the \"library of Anthony Stark, millionaire weapons manufacturer.\"&nbsp; Once Hank and Jan appear (continuing their blatant pill popping from the last issue of <i>Tales to Astonish</i> -- <i>yeesh</i>), they sit down to, as Iron Man points out, \"get to know each other better.\"\n\nMeanwhile we see the Space Phantom arriving on Earth, shaped like a \"strange egg-like object\" where he plainly indicates it's been \"weeks of faster-than-light travel.\"&nbsp; He lands on Earth and then <i>immediately</i> sets out to \"find the Avengers and begin Phase Two.\"&nbsp; He's arrived on Earth to destroy the Avengers so that \"no power on Earth can stop the total invasion by my people!\"\n\nWe learn the Space Phantom can take on the form of anyone, while at the same time banishing that person to \"limbo\" until such time as he assumes <i>another</i> form.&nbsp; Because he's been observing the Avengers \"for months from space,\" he knows they're meeting in Tony Stark's mansion.&nbsp; And even though he's detected intruding, the first person to confront him is the Hulk, whose form he immediately takes, which allows him to stir up a brawl between the Avengers, all to stir up trouble in their ranks.\n\nLater, while out on the street, he is approached by Rick Jones, who obviously knows him.&nbsp; Through Rick, the Space Phantom learns that the Hulk has another identity which he apparently did not know.&nbsp; He also learns that the Hulk travels from place to place by taking tremendous leaps.&nbsp; After he reveals his power to Rick Jones, he uses the Hulk's powerful legs to carry him back toward the Avengers.\n\nOn the way, however, he passes by one of Stark International's facilities, where they are about to test an \"anti-missile gun.\"&nbsp; He decides to destroy it to cause more mayhem, for which the Hulk will ultimately be blamed, and as a result, Iron Man soon flies out to confront him.&nbsp; During the ensuing battle, the Space Phantom assumes another shape (a wasp), leaving Iron Man now facing the <i>real</i> Hulk, but being completely unaware of the change.\n\nMeanwhile, Rick Jones has somehow already \"reached the nearest member of his nationwide Teen Brigade\" and sends out an urgent warning to Giant-Man.&nbsp; Now alerted to the conflict, it doesn't take long for Giant-Man and the Wasp to join in the fray between Iron Man and the Hulk, and with their help, they settle everyone down temporarily.\n\nBut the Space Phantom is still nearby (in wasp form) and attacks the Wasp, leading the others to rush to her aid inside one of Stark's factory buildings, there surprising Giant-Man and assuming his form.&nbsp; But <i>this</i> time, the switch is witnessed by both the Wasp and the Hulk, so his ruse is discovered!\n\nAs \"Giant-Man\" and the Hulk do battle, the Wasp is put in position to run for help, even as Iron Man <i>also </i>joins in the fray.&nbsp; The Space Phantom, hoping that the Hulk doesn't see, then assumes Iron Man's form, causing the real Giant-Man to return from limbo.&nbsp; But the Hulk <i>did</i> see the transformation and calls out a warning to Giant-Man to attack \"Iron Man.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the Wasp heads straight for the office of Dr. Don Blake, who she's been told can summon Thor.&nbsp; After he arrives, the Wasp fills him in, and they join the others in the factory of Tony Stark.\n\nThor and the Wasp arrive, both independently deducing that the figure of Iron Man is really the Space Phantom.&nbsp; While Thor calls down torrential rain and lightning, the Wasp slips inside the armor to cause mechanical mayhem.&nbsp; But the storm ultimately rusts the Iron Man armor, forcing the Space Phantom to attempt a new identity, this time attempting to assume Thor's shape.\n\nBut in doing so, he finds he has miscalculated.&nbsp; Thor is not a normal human, so the Space Phantom is sent to limbo instead, and moments later, is gone.&nbsp; The real Iron Man returns (his armor still unrusted).\n\nAs the dust settles and they talk about the fighting they did amongst themselves, the Hulk declares that they all hate him deep down and jumps off, quitting the team, just like that!&nbsp; What will the world do now without the Avengers to keep the Hulk in check?",
  "grade_one_liner": "A pileup of plot holes and logic gaps that somehow dares you to keep going.\nYou take note like a rubbernecker, amazed that it ever made it to print.",
  "subtitle": "The Phantom Menace (of Continuity)",
  "review_text": "This was the first time I've dived deep into this issue and realized how pathetic it really is.&nbsp; It was an absolute disaster when it comes to consistency and a dreadful follow-up issue to the solid-enough first one (a surprising drop in quality for a book only a couple of months old).&nbsp; It was so full of plot holes that if the story itself had been even decent, they would have ruined any enjoyment.\n\nAs I was reluctant to litter the plot summary with a bunch of knee-jerk reactions, I instead will just present a list of [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=\"What the--?\"] moments:\n\n[HANG0] * When Hank and Jan arrive on page 2 (popping pills again), no one ever bothers to ask him what happened to Ant-Man.&nbsp; They all just assume that the costumed man next to the Wasp is him (and merely forgot his new name).&nbsp; The <i>cover</i> at least acknowledges that Giant-Man is a new character and points him out to the reader.\n\n[HANG0] * Page 3 indicates that the Space Phantom has been in his egg-shaped spacecraft after \"weeks of faster-than-light travel,\" sent to Earth to \"destroy the Avengers.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only is this yet <i>another</i> alien invasion story, but how in the world has the Space Phantom even <i>heard</i> of the Avengers, [SPAN: severity=error; text=who <i>just formed</i>?]&nbsp; You'd think that if it's taken him weeks of faster-than-light travel, he'd arrive on Earth with little to no information about current events on Earth, particularly the newly-formed group, even though he says on page 4, panel 4, he's been observing them \"for months from space!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=(<i>Huh?</i>  They just <i>formed</i>!&nbsp; It's already been <b><i>months</i></b><i>?</i>)]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(The timeline simply refuses to add up -- either he traveled for weeks or he watched them for months, but the story insists on both.)]\n[BR]\n[BR] All the same, you do at least have to love those eyeballs that Kirby draws him with.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=What a creepy-looking character -- pointed hair and pointed ears to <i>boot</i>!]\n\n[HANG0] * Page 4 suggests that the Space Phantom knows that Iron Man is Tony Stark because he's been observing them from space.&nbsp; (<i>Boy</i>, just like the Skrulls from [LINK:1963:FF_18|FF #18|2026-10-29], this guy must have some <i>truly advanced</i> live-television technology!)\n\n[HANG0] * Thor has no problem calling the Hulk a \"savage\" on page 5.\n[ASSET: center | YouSavage | width=67%]\n\n[HANG0] * Page 7 shows Rick Jones on a New York City street, someone who actually <i>knows</i> the Hulk.&nbsp; But in the previous issue, Rick Jones and the Teen Brigade were in their headquarters, \"in the Southwest, halfway across the country.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=What's he now doing in New York?]&nbsp; (The Teen Brigade is supposed to be a nationwide radio network -- not a teleportation service.)&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Sigh...]\n\n[ASSET: right | DonBlake | width=12%] [HANG0] * Rick can't know the Hulk all <i>that</i> well, because he says that he helps the Hulk \"turn back to [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Doctor Don Blake].\"\n[BR]\n[BR] (<i>Uh</i>...obviously the Hulk isn't the <i>only</i> one Rick seems to know.&nbsp; Don Blake is <i>Thor's</i> alter-ego!)\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I <i>know</i> this just a <i>mistake</i>, but Artie Simek also letters Thor.&nbsp; Does he even pay <i>attention</i>?)]\n\n[HANG0] * The Space Phantom learns from Rick Jones that the Hulk has another identity, which seems to surprise him.&nbsp; But if he's really been observing the Avengers \"for months,\" how is it that he knows that Iron Man is Tony Stark while at the same time being ignorant that the Hulk is really Bruce Banner?\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only that, but he also seems surprised to learn that the Hulk travels the countryside by leaping from place to place.&nbsp; Wouldn't you have learned that <i>too</i> during your observations?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(His surveillance seems to work only when the plot needs it.)]\n\n[HANG0] * Even though the \"Hulk\" jumped Rick out to the countryside on page 9 (see the rocks in the background), Rick still manages to make it to the \"nearest member of his nation-wide Teen Brigade\" on page 13, even though page 10 says that the Hulk was only \"a short distance away\" from Stark's missile test, where the fighting broke out between Iron Man and the Hulk.\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only is this <i>amazing</i> timing on Rick's behalf, but take note of how <i>huge</i> that ham radio is on the first panel of page 13.&nbsp; That is some <i>serious</i> setup for a teenager!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\n[HANG0] * Obviously Rick Jones is more informed on the latest news than the Avengers themselves, for on page 13, he sends out a call to \"Giant-Man\" rather than \"Ant-Man\" on his ham radio, but the splash page had the Avengers wondering where <b>Ant-Man</b> was, because they couldn't start their meeting <i>without</i> him.\n\n[HANG0] * But when Giant-Man and the Wasp join the fray, Iron Man calls him \"Giant-Man.\"&nbsp; It's possible that Hank introduced himself that way to the group on page 2 (seeing as he showed up as Giant-Man rather than Ant-Man), so this is one to perhaps let slide.&nbsp; (Still, for a brand‑new team, the identity shuffle feels oddly unremarked.)\n\n[HANG0] * When Space Phantom assumes the form of Giant-Man, Hank still has time to say, \"I'm whirling into another dimension... a shadowy, silent world!\"&nbsp; Wow, he sure can process what he's seeing <i>fast</i> -- so fast that we can still see the bottom half of his body in the panel!&nbsp; It's as if he's describing a scene the artist hasn't finished drawing yet.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Always the egghead scientist, I guess.)]\n[ASSET: center | WordyIdentityTheft | width=67%]\n\n[HANG0] * On page 19, the Wasp is told to wait in the reception area of Dr. Don Blake's office while he sends for Thor.&nbsp; No sooner is he out of sight than he stamps his cane on the ground and becomes Thor, <i>immediately</i> calling out for the Wasp to return to the office.&nbsp; Thor is now standing there (and no sign of Dr. Blake).&nbsp; <i>Seriously</i>?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=How dumb does he think she is?]\n[BR]\n[BR] Plus, it's important to realize that the Hulk <i>only just took off</i> from their earlier meeting.&nbsp; It simply <i>can't</i> have been more than an hour earlier, and Thor has already gone to <i>work</i> as his mortal identity?&nbsp; (To be fair, so did Tony Stark, as we see on page 11.)\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=I guess the thought of an angry Hulk storming out of their meeting onto the streets of New York City back on page 7 wasn't worth worrying about -- certainly not enough to <b>stop them from heading to the office!</b>]\n\n[HANG0] * After the Wasp fills in Thor on what's been going on, Thor flies off shouting [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"<b>Death</b> to the Space Phantom!\"]&nbsp; <i>Wow!&nbsp; </i>No overreaction <i>there</i>!&nbsp; (It's a jarring escalation for a villain whose biggest crime so far is impersonation... <i>well</i>... and maybe some Hulk-level carnage...)\n\n[HANG0] * Page 20 shows Thor and the Wasp arriving at the scene of the battle, both deducing that the \"Iron Man\" below must be the Space Phantom.&nbsp; Though <i>correct</i>, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=no explanation is provided for how they determine this] other than \"Iron Man is winning against the Hulk and Giant-Man, so therefore, he <i>has</i> to be the Space Phantom!\"&nbsp; It's detective work by process of convenience.\n[BR]\n[BR] (At least the fact they know there <b><i>is</i></b> a being called the Space Phantom can be explained, since he identified himself on page 16, right in front of both the Wasp and the Hulk.)\n\n[HANG0] * Page 21 has Thor say, \"<i>Not so</i>, thou [SPAN: severity=error; text=braggert]!\" (top-right panel)&nbsp; Artie Simek strikes again and <i>really</i> needs to double-check his work with a dictionary -- the <i>correct</i> spelling is \"[SPAN: severity=info; text=braggart].\"\n\n[HANG0] * Page 21 also shows the Iron Man armor being hit with the full intensity of Thor's storm, and by the very next panel, the armor [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=rusts!]&nbsp; (<b><i>What?</i></b><i>  </i>This would seem to be a [SPAN: severity=error; text=serious design flaw] in the armor suit, and I cannot believe Stark would really be that dumb, <i>especially</i> for a guy introduced as a genius engineer.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Forget the <i>Melter</i> from last month's <i>Tales of Suspense</i>!&nbsp; It seems that Iron Man would just as easily succumb to <i>any</i> water-based villain -- perhaps someone like the Sub-Mariner!)\n\n[ASSET: left | ThreeToes | width=17%] And <i>none</i> of this even includes the problem with the Hulk's <b>toes</b>.&nbsp; On the splash page, he's <i>clearly</i> shown to have only three toes on each foot (and there's absolutely no doubt about the count here --- it's <i>quite</i> obvious).&nbsp; As I'd never noticed this before now, I looked at the previous issue of <i>Avengers</i> to compare.\n[BR]\n[BR] Kirby doesn't provide any close-ups on the feet in that issue, but you can tell he has at least 4 present, and going back even farther, in <i>Incredible Hulk</i> issue 5 ([LINK:1962:HULK_5_hulk1|HULK #5hulk]), the opening panel of the second feature story, Kirby draws him with 5 toes.\n[BR]\n[BR] In [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6], Steve Ditko draws at least one panel where you can see 5 toes as well, so while no one really provides a great close-up, in all depictions before this one where you get at least <i>some</i> close-up on the feet, the Hulk is drawn with more than 3, so Jack Kirby seems to have gotten confused here!&nbsp; (And once you notice it, you can't <i>un‑see</i> it.)\n\n[ASSET: left | ToesPage9 | width=14%] [ASSET: right | ToesPage10 | width=20%] But even within this very issue, Kirby seems to go back and forth between 3 and 4 toes per foot.\n[BR]\n[BR] Don't believe me?&nbsp; Look at the last panel on page 9 and compare to the first one on page 10 (I've provided the panels here).&nbsp; It does seem like for the <i>most part</i>, he draws him with 4.\n\nBut enough of this...!&nbsp; (Except to say that this was called out in Roger Gilman's letter, printed in AVEN #5 as well, to which the response was a callout to the readership to vote on how many toes \"ol' green skin\" should have.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPut all these things together, and there is but one inevitable conclusion to reach:&nbsp; this magazine took leave of its senses almost from the very first page.&nbsp; It is remarkable how an issue filled with such inconsistencies made it to print.\n\nLet's be honest:&nbsp; these early Marvel books have routinely showed growing pains.\n[BR] <b><i>Fair enough</i></b> -- but this issue feels <i>uniquely</i> scrambled.\n\nIt's at least nice to know that, right from the very start, Tony Stark provides the Avengers' funding and headquarters.&nbsp; Not only that, but page 10 of this story comes right out and says that Iron Man is on Stark's payroll.&nbsp; That notion was only <i>just introduced</i> in last month's [LINK:1963:TOS_47_iron0|TOS #47|2027-01-03], and already, it seems to be common knowledge.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n[ASSET: left | CreepyAlien | width=21%] I'll at least give Kirby some love for the Space Phantom's appearance -- that guy can sure draw memorable villains!&nbsp; (Even when the story around them wobbles.)\n[BR]\n[BR] But in addition to all the irritating inconsistencies I listed out above (and as if to add insult to injury), after the Avengers finally <i>do</i> prevail over the alien Space Phantom, the question of the planned invasion (the one Space Phantom himself mentions on page 3 when we first meet him) [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=is never resolved.]&nbsp; If his people were indeed planning an invasion of Earth, did his disappearance stop it?\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=It seems Stan and Jack simply don't care to address this.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(They <i>got</i> our 12 cents!&nbsp; We've been <i>had</i>, and life goes on!&nbsp; <b><i>Argh!</i></b>)]\n\n[ASSET: right | HulkQuits | width=11%] More to the point, it's 1963, and I guess aliens are so commonplace now that [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=none of the Avengers] even bother to wonder if the Space Phantom is merely the first of <i>many</i> possible invaders.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=On the contrary, they seem <i>far</i> more concerned about the Hulk quitting the team!]\n\nIt is this last aspect that, in my mind, prevents this issue from getting an even lower grade, as you <i>have</i> to admit that Stan and Jack genuinely surprise here.&nbsp; I'm <i>convinced</i> that [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=not one reader] anticipated that the Hulk would be gone after only two issues of the magazine!\n\nI don't know if this was the plan all along by Lee and Kirby, or if it was something they decided on later in the couple of months of real time between issues 1 and 2 to think about and decide if the Hulk could ever really be a good member.\n\nDon't forget that Giant-Man was introduced in last month's <i>Tales to Astonish</i> ([LINK:1963:TTA_49_giant0|TTA #49|2026-12-19]), and they may have simply thought that his presence (together with Thor and Iron Man) made the Hulk and his brute strength <i>expendable</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=(It could very well have been as simple as <i>that</i>.)]\n\nRegardless of the rationale, you have to admit this is a twist no one saw coming, and at least for that, the book did have something positive to offer in a tale that otherwise contained so many glaring inconsistencies that it seemed <i>sabotaged</i>.&nbsp; As a story, it was [SPAN: severity=error; text=just...downright...terrible!]\n\nHaving done some writing of my own, I know how deep into the weeds of a story the author can get -- in this case writing panel-by-panel.&nbsp; You can <i>easily</i> lose track of the larger matter of how little real time has elapsed.\n\nThis story completely forgets the fact that it (the whole story) elapses in seemingly an hour or less.&nbsp; There's simply no way all these other side events could have taken place at the same time!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=Just no way!]&nbsp; It's as if each character is operating in a completely different time zone!\n\nThis is at the very least a finalist for 1963's dreaded [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Trash Heap of the Year] award.\n\nIf nothing else, it seems to be the one riddled with the most plot holes -- a comic so <i>spectacularly inconsistent</i> that it earns its already‑low grade only because the sheer incompetence becomes [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>perversely fascinating</i>].\n\nI kept going, not out of enjoyment, but out of a <i>morbid curiosity</i> to see just how far off the rails it could fall.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=It was <i>astonishing</i> to read this!]",
  "decoder_ring": "[ASSET: left | Shellhead | width=11%] The bottom of page 14 shows the Hulk calling Iron Man \"shell-head\" (apparently its first usage).\n[BR]\n[BR] This is a nickname for Iron Man that becomes so popular that the letters page of his own magazine was eventually called [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sock It To Shell-Head] for years.",
  "highlight": "The Avengers stumble through a Space Phantom fiasco so messy you can't help rubbernecking the wreck."
}